From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 16 Sep 2026 15:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: lib: clear_user: branch to the loop body, not to its padding

The 8-byte store loop in __arch_clear_user() carries its alignment
directive behind the loop label:

	1:	.p2align 4
	USER(9f, sttr	xzr, [x0])
		add	x0, x0, #8
		subs	x1, x1, #8
		b.hi	1b

`.p2align 4` makes the assembler pad to the next 16-byte boundary, but
`1:` is resolved before that padding, so `1b` is the address of the first
NOP rather than the address of the first store. The padding is inside the
loop and runs on every iteration. How much of it there is depends on where
the function happens to land: the assembler emits zero to three NOPs, so
the loop costs four to seven instructions per eight bytes cleared, and an
unrelated change elsewhere in the kernel can move it from one end of that
range to the other.

Move the directive above the label. The padding then sits in front of the
branch target and executes once, on entry from the size check above it,
while the loop body keeps the 16-byte alignment the directive was added
for. The generated loop drops from six instructions to four:

	before				after
	 28: nop			 28: nop
	 2c: nop			 2c: nop
	 30: sttr  xzr, [x0]		 30: sttr  xzr, [x0]
	 34: add   x0, x0, #0x8		 34: add   x0, x0, #0x8
	 38: subs  x1, x1, #0x8		 38: subs  x1, x1, #0x8
	 3c: b.hi  28			 3c: b.hi  30

The ordering changed in fe59e0358d9b ("arm64: lib: Use MOPS for usercopy
routines"). Before it, `.p2align 4` stood in front of SYM_FUNC_START(), so
the loop head inherited its alignment from the function entry and no label
captured the padding. Every arm64 CPU without FEAT_MOPS runs this loop.

MEASURED

  The two loops differ in the branch target and in nothing else: same
  instructions, same padding, same buffer, alternated and repeated, median
  of five. Allwinner A733, 1 MiB buffer, cleared 2000 times per run, fixed
  clocks:

    Cortex-A55, 1794 MHz   4.43 -> 6.64 GiB/s   (+49.9%)
    Cortex-A76, 2002 MHz   9.62 -> 11.91 GiB/s  (+23.8%)

  The A55 figure is the instruction count on its own: six instructions per
  eight bytes against four is 1.5x, and an in-order core pays it in full.

  The same ratio shows up in the kernel path. Reading /dev/zero on this
  kernel retires 795k instructions per MiB inside __arch_clear_user() where
  a 6.6 kernel built from the same config retires 541k, with equal IPC and
  equal cache miss rates; throughput is 23% (Cortex-A55) to 25%
  (Cortex-A76) lower.

UPSTREAM STATUS

  Not posted. The symptom is known upstream and the cause is not: Luke
  Yang reported a ~30% /dev/zero read regression on 2025-11-21 and read it
  as the loop straddling an I-cache line, proposing `.p2align 6` in front
  of SYM_FUNC_START(). Will Deacon objected that nothing makes this
  function special. The thread stopped there and mainline is unchanged as
  of 2026-09-16.

  That report is consistent with this patch and explains itself through it:
  aligning the function entry fixes the symptom by accident, because it
  moves the loop head onto a 16-byte boundary and the assembler then has no
  padding left to emit. The next change in front of the loop re-rolls it.

  Luke Yang, "[PATCH] arm64: clear_user: align __arch_clear_user() to
  128B for I-cache efficiency", LKML, 2025-11-21,
